WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.760
With almost 500
sessions in Next 2018,

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.010
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.410
So if you didn't see
"BeyondCorp Beyond Google,"

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.290
then stay tuned, because
here is the rewind.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.278
[JINGLE]

00:00:15.939 --> 00:00:18.230
If you're a large scale
company with lots of employees,

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.180
devices, and
applications, then chances

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.200
are your IT department
talks a lot about security.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.200
And it's understandable.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.880
Given our technology
age, there's

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.040
lots of attack vectors
to your company's data.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.830
This is why about
10 years ago, Google

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.500
decided we wanted
to get rid of VPNs,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.110
deciding that no network
was actually safe,

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:37.670
and the only way
to improve security

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.010
was to evaluate each
request as it happened.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.700
And this was the
beginning of BeyondCorp.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.580
At its core, BeyondCorp is
comprised of three things

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.350
that the enterprise can control.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.340
First, policies that let you
define what and who should

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.290
or shouldn't have access.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.620
Second, the set of attributes
about the specific request,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.110
giving you important context.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.530
And third, applications and
data that need protecting.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.180
With this in place,
BeyondCorp lets

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.120
us ask various security
questions per request

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.780
at various stages.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.800
For example, is this
employee a real user?

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.510
Is the device they
are using known

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:16.430
or has it been compromised?

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.440
Is the network path vulnerable?

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.060
And finally, given
all that data,

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.730
should the user be allowed
to access the applications?

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.860
This is done through a
set of four main tools.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.050
First, the identity tools,
which let you do things

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.270
like keep a database
of users, their job

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.640
roles, and their access
levels, along with the ability

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.770
to have secondary credentials
and short lived sessions.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.400
Next, endpoint verification,
which boils down

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.290
to having a database
of your devices,

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.564
understanding what operating
systems are supported,

00:01:41.564 --> 00:01:42.980
and what device
information can be

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.800
used to make access decisions.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.600
Then there's the
Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.540
which allows admins to
configure which people have

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:53.160
access to which applications.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.890
And finally, the
control engine, which

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.990
contains a list of
these items mashed up

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.510
with buckets of access
that allow for easy scale

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:03.100
to thousands of applications
in your environment.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.650
But let's look at something
a little more specific.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.479
Let's say an employee is sitting
inside Google's headquarters.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.970
At the identity level,
we know it's Sofia

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.416
and they're logged in.

00:02:13.416 --> 00:02:15.970
We know the MacBook, because
we have its device ID

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.940
and that it's running the
operating system that we trust.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.030
We also know they're
coming from the right IP

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.780
and that it's a
corporate device.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.470
So we know they can access the
data they need and the proxy

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.450
applies that policy.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.670
This person on this
device has access

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.070
to the bug systems
and the admin tools.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.440
What's interesting is how
this changes when Sophia

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.420
moves to the coffee shop.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.250
We still know it's
Sophia, but they're

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.280
using their own tablet, which
we don't know anything about

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.910
and don't trust the
operating system.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.670
Our rules engine then notes
that, in the current state,

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:50.010
Sofia can't access
the bug system.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.660
But in that current
state, Sophia

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.910
can access some systems
like calendar and email,

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.370
giving us the right
level of access

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:59.880
to the person and security
to the corporate data.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.380
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.280
check out the link in
the description below.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.696
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube channel

00:03:05.696 --> 00:03:07.717
for more great "Next
Rewind" content.

00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:08.550
Thanks for watching.

